## Limits

| Limit | Value |
| - | - |
| Requests per file | 50,000 |
| File size | 200 MB (209,715,200 bytes) |
| Line size | 4 MB (4,194,304 bytes) |
| Filename | 1 to 255 bytes |
| Output and error files, combined | 8 GB. A batch over this ends `failed` with the `output_too_large` error. |

Your organization can also have a limited number of unfinished batch requests at once, based on its [usage tier](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/admin/rate-limits-usage-tiers.md). Each line of an input file is one request. The count drops as requests finish.

| Usage tier | Unfinished batch requests |
| - | - |
| Tier 0 | 50,000 |
| Tier 1 | 100,000 |
| Tier 2 | 200,000 |
| Tier 3 | 300,000 |
| Tier 4 | 500,000 |
| Tier 5 | 500,000 |
| Tier 6 and above | 1,000,000 |

A create that would go over the limit returns `429` with the current count and limit. Wait for earlier batches to finish, or submit a smaller file.

## Billing

There is no separate batch price. OpenAI models run on the `flex` [service tier](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/agent-api/models.md#service-tiers) where the model lists it. Other models run on the standard tier at their normal price. Starting 20 to 24 hours after the batch was created, requests that start or resume run on the standard tier so they can finish before the deadline. Each result's `response.body.service_tier` shows the tier that served it. Tokens bill at the tier that served each step. Tool calls bill at their normal price.

Requests that never started or that expired are not billed. A request stopped while it was running can bill for the steps it already ran.

Creating a batch requires API credits. If your credits run out while a batch runs, the batch pauses. Requests refused for credits do not fail. The batch resumes after you add credits. The batch deadline does not change while it is paused. Requests still waiting at the deadline expire with `batch_expired`.

## Errors

| Status | When |
| - | - |
| `400` | Invalid request, an invalid input file line, an unsupported option such as `expires_after` or `output_expires_after`, a missing or expired `input_file_id` on create, or an `Idempotency-Key` reused with a different request. |
| `402` | Your API credits are exhausted. |
| `403` | Your organization has Zero Data Retention. |
| `404` | The batch or file was not found, or belongs to another organization. Expired files also return `404`. |
| `409` | The batch is `finalizing`, `completed`, `expired`, or `failed` and cannot be cancelled. |
| `413` | The file is larger than 200 MB. |
| `429` | Rate limit, unfinished request limit, or too many uploads in progress. Retry later. |
| `503` | The service is temporarily unavailable. Retry the request. |

