          "mixed": "Praise and complaints in one review",
          "negative": "Mostly dissatisfied"
        }
      },
      "severity": {
        "type": "score",
        "instructions": "How severe is the reported problem?",
        "criteria": ["Cosmetic", "Inconvenient", "Product unusable"]
      }
    }
  }'
```

**4. Read the answers**

The response has one answer per question, under the name you gave it. This example shows the response structure; the exact probabilities depend on the model that serves the request:

```json theme={null}
{
  "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
  "answers": {
    "defect": {
      "type": "noul",
      "noul": 0.9424522889347015
    },
    "sentiment": {
      "type": "choice",
      "choice": "mixed",
      "confidence": 0.9255246944002182,
      "probabilities": {
        "positive": 0.020649883775315993,
        "mixed": 0.9503497962668123,
        "negative": 0.02900031995787183
      }
    },
    "severity": {
      "type": "score",
      "score": 1.7838686319784252,
      "confidence": 0.7838686319784252,
      "legend": {
        "0": "Cosmetic",
        "1": "Inconvenient",
        "2": "Product unusable"
      },
      "probabilities": {
        "0": 0.008423954913615923,
        "1": 0.199283458194343,
        "2": 0.7922925868920411
      }
    }
  },
  "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 367,
    "output_tokens": 3
  }
}
```

Read it like this:

* **`defect`**: a 94% probability that the review reports a defect. Compare `noul` against a threshold you choose; values near 0.5 mean the model is unsure.
* **`sentiment`**: `mixed` is the option with the highest probability (95%). `probabilities` covers every option you defined and sums to about 1, so you can see how close the runner-up came.
* **`severity`**: `score` is the probability-weighted average of the level indices, so 1.78 sits between `Inconvenient` (1) and `Product unusable` (2), closer to 2. `legend` maps each index back to your rubric, and `probabilities` shows the full distribution over levels.

`confidence` on `choice` and `score` answers is computed from `probabilities` and runs from 0 to 1. It is not the top probability:

* **`choice`**: `(p_top - 1/n) / (1 - 1/n)`, where `p_top` is the highest probability and `n` is the number of options. It is 0 when every option is equally likely and 1 when one option has all the probability. In the example above, `sentiment` has a top probability of 0.95 across three options, so its `confidence` is (0.95 - 1/3) / (2/3) = 0.93.
* **`score`**: `1 - spread / baseline`, with a floor of 0. `spread` is the probability-weighted distance from the most likely level: the sum of `p_i × |i - top|` over all levels. `baseline` is the average of `|i - top|` over all levels, unweighted. In the example above, the most likely level is 2, so `spread` is 0.0084 × 2 + 0.1993 × 1 = 0.216, `baseline` is (2 + 1 + 0) / 3 = 1, and `confidence` is 0.78.

A `choice` with one option or a `score` with one level has a `confidence` of 1.

In our tests on earlier decision models, identical requests usually returned identical numbers. Occasionally they differed in the second decimal place, so set thresholds with some margin. `model` echoes the model name you sent.

## Question types

Every question in a request refers to the same `state`, which can be a string, an object, or an array. You name each question, and the response uses the same names. Each question has a `type`, `instructions` (what to decide), and, depending on the type, `criteria`.

### `noul`: yes or no

Ask a question or state something to check. Give `instructions`, `criteria`, or both; `criteria` defines what counts as yes and what counts as no. A `noul` with neither returns `400`.

```json theme={null}
{
  "type": "noul",
  "instructions": "Does the review report a product defect?",
  "criteria": {
    "true": "Something is broken or not working.",
    "false": "Normal wear or personal preference."
  }
}
```

The answer is `noul`, the probability of yes or true, from 0 to 1.

### `choice`: one of your options

`criteria` maps each option name to a description of when it applies. Use `null` as the description to let the name speak for itself. A question accepts 1 to 255 options.

```json theme={null}
{
  "type": "choice",
  "instructions": "Which team should handle this ticket?",
  "criteria": {
    "billing": "Charges, refunds, and invoices",
    "shipping": "Delivery status and lost packages",
    "other": null
  }
}
```

The answer has `choice` (the option with the highest probability), `probabilities` (one value per option, summing to about 1), and `confidence`.

### `score`: a level on an ordered rubric

`criteria` is an ordered array of level descriptions. The index in the array is the level's score, starting at 0. A question accepts up to 10 levels. Use at least two: with a single level there is nothing to decide, so the answer is always a score of 0 with probability 1.

```json theme={null}
{
  "type": "score",
  "instructions": "How severe is the reported problem?",
  "criteria": ["Cosmetic", "Inconvenient", "Product unusable"]
}
```

The answer has `score` (the probability-weighted average of the level indices, which can fall between two levels), `legend` (each index, as a string, mapped back to your rubric entry), `probabilities` (one value per level, keyed like `legend`), and `confidence`.

### Images in `state`

`state` can contain images. Write each image as an OpenAI-style part, `{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,..."}}`, anywhere in `state`: as an array item or as a value inside an object, at any depth. The model reads `state` as serialized JSON, with each image placed exactly where its part appears, so the order you write things in is the order the model sees them.

In an array, next to text:

```json theme={null}
{
  "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
  "state": [
    "Which color is the square?",
    {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo..."}}
  ],
  "questions": {
    "color": {"type": "choice", "instructions": "What color is the square?", "criteria": {"red": null, "blue": null, "green": null}}
  }
}
```

As a value inside an object:

```json theme={null}
"state": {
  "caption": "Product photo from the listing",
  "photo": {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/jpeg;base64,/9j/4AAQ..."}}
}
```

If the content is just an image, pass the image part itself as `state`. You don't need to add an empty text field:

```json theme={null}
"state": {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo..."}}
```

* PNG, JPEG, and WebP data URLs are accepted. The API does not fetch image URLs: an `http` or `https` image URL returns `400`.
* Up to 8 images per question. More returns `400`.
* The API reads images in 32 × 32 pixel tiles. Keep each image at or under 2,048 tiles: round the width and the height to the nearest multiple of 32 and keep (width / 32) × (height / 32) at or under 2,048. 1440 × 1440 and 2048 × 1024 fit; 1600 × 1310 does not. A larger image does not return `400`: the request waits about a minute and then returns `504`. Resize before you send.
* Image tokens count toward `usage.input_tokens` and the input limit like text. In our tests on earlier decision models, an image cost about 1,000 input tokens per megapixel.

### Request limits

| Limit | Value |
| - | - |
| Questions per request | At least 1, each with a non-empty name. With `mode: "web_grounding"`, at most 100; more returns `400` |
| Options per `choice` | 1 to 255 |
| Levels per `score` | 1 to 10 |
| Input tokens | Under 256K for the `state`, its images, and each question. On `pplx-decider-v1.2`, questions that don't fit together are answered in groups; `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2` answers each question on its own. See [Grouping questions in one request](#grouping-questions-in-one-request) |
| Request body | 32 MiB |
| Images per question | Up to 8 |
| Image size | 2,048 tiles of 32 × 32 pixels per image, for example 1440 × 1440 or 2048 × 1024 |

`state` must be a string, an object, or an array; `null` returns `400`. `score` levels follow the same rule. `choice` descriptions can also be `null`. The top-level fields are `model`, `state`, `questions`, and the optional [`mode`](#web-grounding) and [`max_staleness`](#control-page-freshness). An unknown top-level field returns `400`.

## Web grounding

Set `mode` to `"web_grounding"` when the answer depends on facts that are not in your `state`, such as recent events or public facts about a company, product, or person. Before the model answers, the API runs one [Fast Search](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/search/fast-search.md) per question and adds the top results to the `state` as evidence. Without `mode`, the model answers from your `state` alone.

You can also [pass a page URL as `state`](#fetch-a-page-from-a-url) to fetch its text before searching.

```python Python theme={null}
from perplexity import Perplexity

client = Perplexity(timeout=30.0)

response = client.decisions.create(
    model="pplx-decider-latest",
    state="Claim: The James Webb Space Telescope launched in December 2021.",
    questions={
        "supported": {
            "type": "noul",
            "instructions": "Is the claim true?",
        },
    },
    # The library has no `mode` parameter yet, so send it in the request body.
    extra_body={"mode": "web_grounding"},
)

print(response.answers["supported"].noul)  # probability of yes, 0 to 1

# The library has no `search_results` field yet, so read it from the dict.
for result in response.to_dict()["search_results"]:
    print(result["url"])
```

```typescript Typescript theme={null}
import Perplexity from "@perplexity-ai/perplexity_ai";

const client = new Perplexity({ timeout: 30_000 });

const response = await client.decisions.create({
  model: "pplx-decider-latest",
  state: "Claim: The James Webb Space Telescope launched in December 2021.",
  questions: {
    supported: {
      type: "noul",
      instructions: "Is the claim true?",
    },
  },
  // The SDK types have no `mode` field yet; the spread sends it in the request body.
  ...{ mode: "web_grounding" },
});
const { supported } = response.answers;

if (supported?.type === "noul") console.log(supported.noul);  // probability of yes, 0 to 1

// The SDK types have no `search_results` field yet; the field is in the response.
const { search_results } = response as typeof response & {
  search_results: { title: string; url: string; snippet: string }[];
};
for (const result of search_results) console.log(result.url);
```

```bash cURL theme={null}
curl -X POST https://api.perplexity.ai/v1/decisions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PERPLEXITY_API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
    "mode": "web_grounding",
    "state": "Claim: The James Webb Space Telescope launched in December 2021.",
    "questions": {
      "supported": {
        "type": "noul",
        "instructions": "Is the claim true?"
      }
    }
  }' | jq
```

The response adds `search_results`, the evidence the model saw. One of the five results, snippet shortened:

```json theme={null}
{
  "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
  "answers": {
    "supported": { "type": "noul", "noul": 0.9983307784830786 }
  },
  "usage": { "input_tokens": 1663, "output_tokens": 1 },
  "search_results": [
    {
      "title": "NASA - NSSDCA - Spacecraft - Details",
      "url": "https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=2021-130A",
      "snippet": "2021-130A\nDescription\nThe James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) is a large infrared (IR) optimized space observatory ... JWST launched on 25 December 2021 ...",
      "last_updated": "2026-11-04"
    }
  ]
}
```

| Field | Type | Meaning |
| - | - | - |
| `title` | string | Page title, up to 300 characters |
| `url` | string | Page URL, normalized. Each URL appears once |
| `snippet` | string | Page text, up to 2,000 characters per snippet. When several searches return the same URL, their snippets are joined by a `---` line |
| `date` | string, optional | Publication date, `YYYY-MM-DD` |
| `last_updated` | string, optional | Last update date, `YYYY-MM-DD` |

`search_results` is `[]` when no search evidence was added. Responses without `mode` don't include it.

### Fetch a page from a URL

With `mode: "web_grounding"`, pass a page URL as `state` to ask questions about that page. The API replaces the URL in `state` with the page text, then searches as usual. The model sees the page text plus any search evidence.

The text in `state` must be a single `http://`, `https://`, or `www.` URL. A text part such as `[{"type":"text","text":"https://example.com/page"}]` also works. Image parts and their metadata stay in place. A URL mixed with other text, such as `"Read https://example.com/page"`, is searched as text rather than fetched.

```python Python theme={null}
from perplexity import Perplexity

client = Perplexity(timeout=30.0)

response = client.decisions.create(
    model="pplx-decider-latest",
    state="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Webb_Space_Telescope",
    questions={
        "launched": {
            "type": "noul",
            "instructions": "Does the page say Webb launched in December 2021?",
        },
    },
    extra_body={"mode": "web_grounding", "max_staleness": 86400},
)

print(response.answers["launched"].noul)
```

```typescript Typescript theme={null}
import Perplexity from "@perplexity-ai/perplexity_ai";

const client = new Perplexity({ timeout: 30_000 });

const response = await client.decisions.create({
  model: "pplx-decider-latest",
  state: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Webb_Space_Telescope",
  questions: {
    launched: {
      type: "noul",
      instructions: "Does the page say Webb launched in December 2021?",
    },
  },
  ...{ mode: "web_grounding", max_staleness: 86400 },
});

const { launched } = response.answers;
if (launched?.type === "noul") console.log(launched.noul);
```

```bash cURL theme={null}
curl -X POST https://api.perplexity.ai/v1/decisions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PERPLEXITY_API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
    "mode": "web_grounding",
    "max_staleness": 86400,
    "state": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Webb_Space_Telescope",
    "questions": {
      "launched": {
        "type": "noul",
        "instructions": "Does the page say Webb launched in December 2021?"
      }
    }
  }' | jq
```

The response includes `fetched_results` alongside `search_results`. Example response, with the page text shortened:

```json theme={null}
{
  "model": "pplx-decider-latest",
  "answers": {
    "launched": { "type": "noul", "noul": 0.98 }
  },
  "usage": { "input_tokens": 13500, "output_tokens": 1 },
  "search_results": [
    {
      "title": "NASA - NSSDCA - Spacecraft - Details",
      "url": "https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=2021-130A",
      "snippet": "The James Webb Space Telescope launched on December 25, 2021."
    }
  ],
  "fetched_results": [
    {
      "title": "James Webb Space Telescope",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Webb_Space_Telescope",
      "snippet": "The James Webb Space Telescope launched on December 25, 2021."
    }
  ]
}
```

`fetched_results` contains at most one item, with `title`, `url`, and `snippet`. The `snippet` contains the fetched page text, up to 40,000 characters. The `url` is normalized, so it may not match the URL you sent byte for byte. If fetching fails or returns no usable text, `fetched_results` is absent and `state` keeps the original URL.

### Control page freshness

Set `max_staleness` to the maximum age of a fetched page in seconds since its last crawl. The value must be a non-negative integer. For example, `86400` allows a stored copy crawled within the past 24 hours. An older copy triggers a fresh crawl before the page text is used. Omit the parameter to use a stored copy without an age limit.

`max_staleness` applies only to the page fetched from a URL-only `state` with `mode: "web_grounding"`; without `mode` it is accepted and ignored. It does not filter search results.

### How web grounding works

* **Queries:** each question becomes one search query: the question text (its `instructions` and criteria) followed by the start of your `state` text, up to 256 bytes. With `state` text, the question part gets at most 128 bytes. Images are not searched. Questions with the same query share one search.
* **Questions:** at most 100 per grounded request. More returns `400` before any search runs.
* **Evidence:** each search returns up to 5 results. The API drops duplicate URLs and keeps up to 10 results, or one per query when there are more than 10 queries, up to 100. A string `state` gets the results after an `Evidence:` line, an array `state` gets them as extra items, and an object `state` becomes an array of the object followed by the results.
* **Time:** searches run up to 10 at a time, with 1 second each and 3 seconds in total. A URL fetch runs before search and adds up to about 250 ms. A search that runs out of time adds no results.
* **Failures:** if no search returns a result, or no question has text to search, the model answers from the `state`, including any fetched page text. If some searches fail, the API uses the others.
* **Privacy:** the queries contain your question text and the start of your `state` text, and go to the Perplexity Search API.

### Web grounding cost

* **Searches:** \$0.001 each (\$1 per 1,000), so at most \$0.10 for a request with 100 questions. See [Pricing](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/getting-started/pricing.md#web-grounding) for which searches are billed.
* **URL fetches:** each successful fetch costs \$0.001 in addition to searches. A failed fetch is not billed.
* **Input tokens:** fetched page text and search evidence are billed as input tokens and included in `usage.input_tokens`. Both count toward the 256K input limit.
* **Grouping:** on `pplx-decider-v1.2`, each group of questions counts the evidence again. On `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2`, each question counts it again. See [Grouping questions in one request](#grouping-questions-in-one-request).

## Models

The Decisions API has two decision models. They take the same request and return the same response, so switching is a one-line change to `model`.

| | `pplx-decider-v1.2` | `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2` |
| - | - | - |
| **Alias** | `pplx-decider-latest` | `pplx-decider-flash-latest` |
| **Parameters** | 27B | 4B |
| **Input price** | \$0.02 per 1M tokens | \$0.01 per 1M tokens |
| **Output tokens** | Free | Free |
| **Input** | Text and images | Text and images |
| **Context window** | 256K tokens | 256K tokens |
| **Options per `choice` / levels per `score`** | 255 / 10 | 255 / 10 |

The two models differ in size, price, and how they answer several questions in one request. Everything else in [Request limits](#request-limits) applies to both.

### When to use each

* **`pplx-decider-v1.2`:** the larger model and the default. Start here, especially for nuanced judgments, such as grading against a detailed rubric or checking a claim against long source text, or where a wrong answer is costly.
* **`pplx-decider-flash-v1.2`:** consider it for high-volume decisions with clear criteria, such as routing, labeling, or filtering, where half the input price matters. You can send several questions in one request, but Flash answers each on its own and bills the `state` once per question; see [Grouping questions in one request](#grouping-questions-in-one-request).

To decide, run both models on a sample of your own labeled data and compare the results at the thresholds you plan to use. Keep Flash only if it meets your accuracy bar on that sample.

### Grouping questions in one request

**`pplx-decider-v1.2` groups questions.** All questions in a request are answered together in one pass. Each question sees the `state` and every other question, and the `state` is billed once. Grouping also changes the answers, which can help or hurt:

* **Grouping helps when the questions are facets of one decision**, such as several attributes of the same item, multi-label "select all that apply" decisions, or the steps of one routing decision. It can also help when comparing candidates is informative, for example multi-hop evidence or duplicate detection.
* **Grouping can hurt when the questions are independent candidates competing for the same judgement**, such as one question per document, tool, or function call. Score those in separate requests.
* **If you're unsure, send one candidate per request**, since a wrong grouping costs more than a missed one.
* The same question can score differently depending on what it's grouped with. Keep the grouping fixed when you compare runs or tune thresholds.

On `pplx-decider-v1.2`, if the `state` plus all questions goes over the input limit, the API splits the questions into consecutive groups and answers each group separately. Each group counts the `state` again in `usage.input_tokens`, so the `state` is billed once per group. The groups run one after another, so a split request takes longer and can reach the `504` in [Timeouts](#timeouts). If the `state` plus a single question goes over the input limit, the request returns `400`.

**`pplx-decider-flash-v1.2` is built for one question per request.** Answering questions together lowered accuracy on the 4B model, so if you send several, each question is answered on its own with the `state` and doesn't see the other questions. The `state` is sent and billed again for every question. Three questions about a 10,000-token `state` bill about 30,000 input tokens.

For more than one question about the same `state`, consider `pplx-decider-v1.2`. It bills the `state` once, so with three or more questions it usually costs less than Flash, even at twice the price per token.

### Model names

Set `model` on every request, using either the versioned name or the alias. An alias follows the newest version of its model: `pplx-decider-latest` and `pplx-decider-flash-latest` can move to a newer decision model over time. When a version is retired, its name routes to the version that replaces it, and the [changelog](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/resources/changelog.md) announces the change. If your application compares probabilities against thresholds, revalidate those thresholds when the model changes. `perplexity/pplx-decider-latest` and `perplexity/pplx-decider-flash-latest` are also accepted and select the same models as their unprefixed aliases.

The response `model` field echoes the name you sent, so a request for `pplx-decider-latest` reports `pplx-decider-latest`, not the version that served it.

Existing requests that name `pplx-decider-v1.1-27b` or `pplx-decider-v1-27b` continue to work. They select `pplx-decider-v1.2` and bill at its rate.

A missing or unknown model returns `400`:

```json theme={null}
{"error": {"code": null, "message": "Invalid model 'pplx-decidr-latest'. Permitted models can be found in the documentation at https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/getting-started/models.", "param": null, "type": "invalid_request_error"}}
```

## Endpoint and authentication

| Operation | Endpoint |
| - | - |
| **Answer questions** | `POST https://api.perplexity.ai/v1/decisions` |

Send the key as `Authorization: Bearer <PERPLEXITY_API_KEY>` and the body as JSON. A key in an `x-api-key` header is not read, so the request returns `401`. Another method on the endpoint returns `405` with `Allow: POST`, and any other path, including a trailing slash, returns `404`.

## Request ids

Responses carry an `x-request-id` header with a UUID, on success and on most errors. Log it with your results and quote it in support requests. A `401`, a `404`, and a `504` carry no request id.

## Rate limits

By default, each organization can send 10 requests per second to the Decisions API, shared across both models. Each model also has its own token limits:

| Model | Tokens per minute | Tokens per hour |
| :- | -: | -: |
| `pplx-decider-v1.2` | 15,000,000 | 900,000,000 |
| `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2` | 4,000,000 | 240,000,000 |

Token limits use rolling windows. Each question counts as one output token, so input size drives token usage. For higher limits, including for traffic bursts, [contact us](https://perplexity.typeform.com/to/yctmfyVT).

Successful responses carry `x-ratelimit-limit`, `x-ratelimit-remaining`, `x-ratelimit-used`, and `x-ratelimit-reset` (Unix seconds). A request over a limit returns `429` with a `Retry-After` header in seconds. Wait that long, then retry.

## Errors

Most errors return a JSON body with an `error` object. Read `error.message` for the reason and `error.type` for the category; don't branch on `error.code`, which is a string, a number, or `null` depending on the error. A `404` or `405` has an empty body, and a `504` can return an HTML page, so check the status before you parse the body.

```json theme={null}
{"error": {"message": "Noul question must have criteria or instructions", "type": "invalid_request", "code": "400"}}
```

| Status | Why | What to do |
| - | - | - |
| **`400`** | The body is not valid JSON, `model` is missing or unknown, a top-level field is unknown, a field has the wrong type, `mode` is not `"web_grounding"`, a limit in [Request limits](#request-limits) is exceeded (for input tokens, the `state` plus a single question is over the limit), or an image is not a supported data URL | Fix the request; `error.message` names the problem |
| **`401`** | The API key is missing or invalid, or it was sent in `x-api-key` | Send `Authorization: Bearer <PERPLEXITY_API_KEY>` and check that the key is active |
| **`404`** | Wrong path | Use `POST https://api.perplexity.ai/v1/decisions`, with no trailing slash |
| **`405`** | Wrong method | Use `POST` |
| **`413`** | The request body is over 32 MiB | Send less `state`, or split the content across requests |
| **`429`** | Over the request or token limit; see [Rate limits](#rate-limits) | Wait `Retry-After` seconds, then retry |
| **`5xx`** | The model did not answer in time (`504`, after about a minute) or the service failed | Retry with backoff; for large inputs, see [Timeouts](#timeouts) |

## Timeouts

Response time grows with input size. In our tests on September 30, 2026, on an earlier decision model, a request with a few hundred input tokens answered in under 2 seconds, about 90,000 tokens took 5 seconds, about 190,000 tokens took 14 seconds, and just under the input limit took 23 seconds. If the model does not answer in time, the request returns `504`; in our tests that took about a minute.

Set your client timeout to fit the input. The examples on this page use 30 seconds, which covered any request under the input limit on that earlier model; for small inputs, 10 seconds was plenty. In our tests on October 19, 2026, a request with about 248,000 input tokens took 53 seconds on `pplx-decider-v1.2` and 16 seconds on `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2`.

## Pricing

The Decisions API costs \$0.02 per million input tokens on `pplx-decider-v1.2` and \$0.01 per million input tokens on `pplx-decider-flash-v1.2`. Aliases bill at the rate of the model they select. Output tokens are free, and there is no per-request fee. With [web grounding](#web-grounding), each web search or successful URL fetch also costs \$0.001 (\$1 per 1,000); see [Pricing](https://docs.perplexity.ai/docs/getting-started/pricing.md#web-grounding). Input tokens are the `usage.input_tokens` value in each response, so you can compute the cost of a request from the response you already receive. Usage is billed to the organization that owns the API key, like every other Perplexity API.

